From 2b626decbf85bd16e6eb161ed1a5561e0780978c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: shuxueshuxue <98064968+shuxueshuxue@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 19 Nov 2026 06:21:36 -0800
Subject: [PATCH] gugu: step a conversation back to before one of its prompts
 (_session/revert)

Why: a turn the model service refuses stays in the conversation, so every
later turn is refused with it and the session is lost. Gugu offers "rewind
and edit" on a user message: the bridge must cut the conversation back to
before that prompt, in place, and continue from there.

A client that declares _meta["gugu.revert"] gets, on every prompt but the
first of a conversation, the point a revert steps back to: _meta.guguRevert
{ before, files } on the prompt receipt and on the replayed user message
(before = the prompt uuid). The declaration also turns file checkpointing on,
which is why it is an opt-in. _session/revert { sessionId, before,
restoreFiles? } restarts the query with resume + resumeSessionAt at the last
chain entry before the prompt, under the same session id; restoreFiles first
restores the files Claude edited from that prompt on (Query.rewindFiles) and
fails the request if it cannot.

resumeSessionAt alone writes nothing: until the next prompt the transcript
still ends at the removed turns, and a restart of the bridge or a plain
`claude --resume` continues from them (measured: all removed turns came
back). So the revert appends one transcript-only entry at the kept point
(shouldQuery: false, no model turn) and answers once it is on disk. The
model reads that entry with the next prompt; a replay leaves it out.

Upstream: none yet; the ACP has no turn-level rewind (Zed edits a message by
truncating its own thread).

Drop: when the ACP gains a standard turn-level revert that the bridge serves.
